{"text": "ILLINOIS JOURNAL OF MATHEMATICS\nVolume 38, Number 3, Pall 194\n\nKNOTS AND SHELLABLE CELL PARTITIONINGS OF S*\n\nSTEVE ARMENTROUT\n\nA cell partitioning of S? is a finite covering H of S* by 3-cells such that if\n1m is any positive integer and exactly m 3-cells of H intersect, their common\npart is a cell of dimension 4 \u2014m, where cells of negative dimension are\nempty. The 3-cells of a cell partitioning of S* fit together in a staggered,\nbrick-like pattern.\nA cell partitioning H of S? is shellable if and only if there is a counting\n(hy,h3,\"**4h,) of H such that if i is an integer and 1<i<n, then\nhy Uh, U ++ Uh, is a 3-cell. Such a counting is a shelling of H.\nIn this paper, we shall study a connection between knots in S? and\nshellability of cell partitionings of $\u00b0. We shall use these results to construct\nnonshellable cell partitionings of S\u00b0.\nOur results involve the use of the bridge number of a knot in S\u00b0. In\nSection 1 of this paper, we shall review some results concerning knots in S*\nand bridge numbers of knots in $\u00b0. In Section 2, we shall establish the main\nresult of the paper. In Section 3, we shall establish a variant of the main\nresult that is useful in some situations. In Section 4, we shall use the results\nof this paper to construct a nonshellable cell partitioning of S* and, as a\nvariation on that construction, a nest of nonshellable cell partitionings of S\u00b0.\nThroughout this paper, we shall assume that S\u00b0 has its standard piecewise\nlinear structure.\n\u2018The author thanks the referee for suggestions and corrections.\n\n1, Knots in S?\n\nA knot in S\u00b0 is a polygonal simple closed curve in $\u00b0. Two knots k and [\nin S? are of the same knot type in S? if and only if there is an orientation\npreserving PL homeomorphism f: $\u00b0 > S? such that f(k) = 1. A knot in $?\nis trivial if and only if it has the same knot type as the boundary of a\n2-simplex in $\u00b0.\nSuppose C is a 3-cell. Then a is a spanning are of C if and only if a is an\nare in C such that Bd a \u00a2 Bd C and Int a \u00a2 Int C. D is a semispanning disc\nof C if and only if D isa disc in C such that Int D c Int C and D 9 BAC is\nan arc on BdC. The statement that 6 is a straight spanning arc of C means\nthat B is a spanning arc of C and there is a semispanning disc D of C such\nthat B c Bd D. Recall that if B is a polyhedral straight spanning arc of a\n\nReceived February 28, 1991,\n1991 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary S7MSO; Secondary $7M25,\n\n\u00a9 1994 by the Board of Trustees of the Univesity of tins\nManefactared inthe United States of Amerea\n\n347",
